ignment at Charlie, and monitoring against side-channel implementation flaws.\n\nDesign a machine-learning-based automation framework for TF-QKD with the following components, and rigorously establish its performance:\n\n1. Adaptive phase and polarization stabilization. Construct a reinforcement-learning controller (specify state space, action space, reward function, and policy class) whose feedback outperforms classical PID-style feedback in residual phase noise and quantum bit error rate (QBER) under realistic fiber drift models.\n\n2. Proactive environmental compensation. Develop predictive models (e.g., recurrent neural networks, Gaussian processes, or Kalman-style filters with learned dynamics) that forecast temperature-, vibration-, and acoustic-induced channel disturbances sufficiently far in advance to pre-compensate phase and polarization, and quantify the achievable reduction in dead time and key-rate loss.\n\n3. Security-aware anomaly detection. Define a statistical anomaly-detection procedure on observed click statistics, decoy-state yields, and detector responses that, with provably bounded false-alarm probability, flags implementation vulnerabilities (detector blinding, Trojan-horse attacks, source flaws) under a specified adversary model.\n\n4. Real-time protocol optimization. Provide an online optimization algorithm that dynamically tunes protocol parameters (signal/decoy intensities, basis-choice probabilities, post-processing block sizes) in response to estimated channel conditions and detected threats so as to maximize the finite-key composable secret-key rate while maintaining a prescribed security parameter.\n\nThe problem is to give such a framework together with theoretical guarantees on (i) convergence and stability of the learning-based controllers, (ii) composable security of the resulting adaptive protocol, and (iii) demonstrable improvement in long-term operational stability, scalability, and key rate over non-learning baselines.","status